<section class="page-banner"><div class="container"><p class="eyebrow">Project overview</p><h1>About the foundation</h1><p>Understanding the path from a browser request to a rendered CodeIgniter view.</p></div></section>

<section class="section container narrow-content"><div class="content-card"><p class="eyebrow">The MVC flow</p><h2>One request, three clear responsibilities</h2><p>CodeIgniter uses the Model-View-Controller pattern to keep an application organized. Routes decide which controller method responds. Controllers prepare the data and choose a view. Views render the HTML that the browser receives.</p><div class="flow-list"><div class="flow-item"><span>01</span><div><strong>Route</strong><p><code>/customers</code> maps to <code>Customers::index()</code>.</p></div></div><div class="flow-item"><span>02</span><div><strong>Controller</strong><p>The controller prepares five customer records in a PHP array.</p></div></div><div class="flow-item"><span>03</span><div><strong>View</strong><p>The view uses <code>foreach</code> to render each record in a table.</p></div></div></div></div><aside class="note-card"><strong>Why no database yet?</strong><p>This formative assessment focuses on routing, controllers, and views. The static arrays are temporary in-memory data sources for the next module.</p></aside></section>

